<article class="env-lemma" id="0CRL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CRL">Lemma <span data-tag="0CRL">22.23.2</span>.</a> Let $(A, \text{d})$ be a differential graded algebra. Let $M_ n$ be a system of differential graded modules. Then the derived colimit $\text{hocolim} M_ n$ in $D(A, \text{d})$ is represented by the differential graded module $\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits M_ n$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Set $M = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits M_ n$. We have an exact sequence of differential graded modules 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to \bigoplus M_ n \to \bigoplus M_ n \to M \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       by Derived Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/093W" data-tag="093W">13.33.6</a> (applied the underlying complexes of abelian groups). The direct sums are direct sums in $D(\mathcal{A})$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/09QI" data-tag="09QI">22.22.4</a>. Thus the result follows from the definition of derived colimits in Derived Categories, Definition <a href="/tag/090Z" data-tag="090Z">13.33.1</a> and the fact that a short exact sequence of complexes gives a distinguished triangle (Lemma <a href="/tag/09L0" data-tag="09L0">22.23.1</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>